<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Multilingual Self</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Language Is an Important Skill</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Explain complex ideas with simple examples. Communication: Facilitate diverse group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain a complex idea or concept related to language learning, using a real-life example (e.g., "Learning a new language is like learning a new game—at first, it's hard, but with practice, it becomes more fun and easier to play.")
            Can facilitate a discussion to clarify concepts by rephrasing them in simpler terms (e.g., "So, what she means is that knowing multiple languages can help you feel more connected to your community.")
            Can clarify misunderstandings during group discussions about multilingualism (e.g., "I think there is some confusion—being bilingual doesn't just mean speaking two languages well, it also means understanding both cultures better.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students watch a short video clip (1-2 minutes) showing a scenario where someone struggles to understand a complex idea about language learning. After the video, the teacher asks: "What was the main problem in the video?" and "How could the speaker have made their point clearer?" This activates prior knowledge about the importance of effective communication and explanation. Students share brief ideas about how *multilingualism* can aid *communication*.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models explaining a complex idea from the "Multilingual Guide" project (e.g., "cognitive benefits of multilingualism") using a simple analogy. For example: "Learning multiple languages is like cross-training for your brain. Just as an athlete builds different muscles, your brain develops new pathways, which enhances problem-solving skills and mental flexibility." The teacher uses phrases like "To put it simply..." or "What this means is..." The teacher also models pronunciation of /s/ in 'success' and /z/ in 'citizens' within context.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'multilingualism enhances cognitive development,' and then I explain it by saying 'learning new languages helps your brain work better, like a puzzle,' am I simplifying the idea?" (Yes)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I explain something simply, am I trying to make it harder or easier for others to understand?" (Easier)</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Multilingual Self".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students read an excerpt from a blog post or an online article (B1.3 level) titled "The Unexpected Power of Multilingualism for Career and Personal Growth." The text contains relatively complex sentences explaining benefits like *enhanced career opportunities* in global *business* and increased *communication* *proficiency*. It uses vocabulary such as *multilingualism, fluency, prioritize, excel, international*. It also includes opinions on language diversity, which can be reported later.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Discussing language diversity: "Multilingualism allows us to connect with people from different cultures and shows respect for their background." Promoting cultural inclusiveness: "If we all learn even a little of each other's languages, we can make our community more inclusive and understanding."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What are the two main areas where multilingualism offers significant advantages, according to the text?" (e.g., career and personal growth)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match complex statements from the text (e.g., "Cognitive flexibility is a significant byproduct of acquiring multiple languages") with simplified summaries provided by the teacher (e.g., "Learning more languages makes your brain more adaptable.").</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-9 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Explaining complex ideas with simple examples; facilitating diverse group discussions; using Reported Speech for opinions on *multilingualism*, Future Simple for predictions about *career opportunities*, and Modals of ability for language *proficiency*. Vocabulary: *multilingualism, proficiency, communication, career, opportunities, business, education, fluency, skill, said, argued, believe, prioritize, offer, communicate, become, excel, enhance, learn, bilingual, multilingual, proficient, effective, international, diverse, multicultural, necessary, fluent*.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
            <ol>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Rephrasing Sentences:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 complex sentences from the Stage 2 text. Students work individually to rephrase them into simpler terms, using provided sentence starters. E.g., "The article highlighted that individuals with high linguistic *proficiency* often *excel* in *international* *business* environments." Starter: "Basically, the text *said* that if you have good *language* *skills*, you *can* do well in global companies." The teacher reminds students to pronounce /s/ in "success" and /z/ in "citizens" carefully.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-Controlled Practice - Reported Opinions & Predictions:</b> In pairs, students discuss one aspect of their "Multilingual Guide" project (e.g., "Benefits for *education*"). One student explains a benefit in their own words, while the other student practices rephrasing what their partner *said* using reported speech and making a related prediction with the future simple. E.g., Student A: "Knowing multiple languages really helps your brain become more flexible." Student B: "So, what she *said* was that *multilingualism* *can* *enhance* your cognitive *skill*. I *believe* that in the future, *education* systems *will prioritize* teaching multiple languages from an early age."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Clarifying Misunderstandings:</b> Students form small groups (3-4). Each student takes a turn presenting a key benefit from their "Multilingual Guide" project. After each presentation, other group members ask clarifying questions or rephrase the idea if they think it's unclear, practicing phrases like "I think there is some confusion—did you mean...?" or "So, what he means is that knowing multiple languages *will* lead to more *opportunities*." Teacher circulates, providing feedback on pronunciation, especially word stress in 'multilingualism' and 'language learning.'</li>
            </ol></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Multilingual Self".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Multilingual Guide Presentation and Facilitated Q&A". Students work in their established groups to present a chosen section of their "Multilingual Guide" (e.g., "Multilingualism and Career Growth" or "Multilingualism and Social Connections"). Each group member takes responsibility for explaining one aspect of the guide, ensuring clarity for their audience. After the brief presentation, the group collectively facilitates a Q&A session with the class, actively mediating any misunderstandings, rephrasing complex questions, and using reported speech to summarize audience contributions or to refer to points made in their guide. They will *offer* practical, culturally sensitive advice that acknowledges and supports diverse *language learning* backgrounds, using *modals of ability* and *future simple* for predictions related to *opportunities*.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion. Students demonstrate their ability to explain complex ideas simply, rephrase for clarity, facilitate discussion by summarizing others' points, and clarify misunderstandings, integrating the specified grammar (reported speech, future simple, modals) and vocabulary (*multilingualism, opportunities, effective communication, diverse*). They will explicitly address how "Multilingualism allows us to connect with people from different cultures and shows respect for their background."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' group presentations and facilitated Q&A sessions are assessed. The teacher uses an observation checklist, and peers use a simplified rubric to assess their classmates' mediation skills. The assessment focuses on the group's ability to clearly explain concepts from their "Multilingual Guide," effectively rephrase ideas, and successfully facilitate discussion by addressing questions and clarifying misunderstandings.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation of complex ideas with simple examples.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effectiveness in rephrasing concepts for better understanding.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of reported speech, future simple, and modals of ability.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /s/ and /z/, and correct word stress in 'multilingualism,' 'multicultural,' 'language learning.'</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active facilitation of group discussion and clarification of misunderstandings.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (*multilingualism, proficiency, communication, career, opportunities, enhance, diverse*).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates cultural sensitivity in advice or explanations.</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one complex idea about *multilingualism* from our guide that I can now explain in a simple way?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did I help clarify a point or facilitate understanding during our group discussion today?"</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">6-8 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of explaining or clarifying ideas about *multilingualism* today, and how did I overcome it?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How *can* I use the *skill* of mediation in other areas of my life, especially when discussing important topics with others?"</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of explaining complex ideas and facilitating discussions worked well? Were students able to use reported speech, future simple, and modals effectively to mediate information? What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks, particularly focusing on promoting cultural inclusiveness and respecting diverse language backgrounds? How can I ensure all students feel comfortable *communicating* their ideas and mediating discussions?</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> (This is Lesson 5, so it's the culmination of the theme. The connection will be to the next unit's theme.) This lesson concludes our theme "Language Is an Important Skill." The next theme will build on students' collaborative and *communication* *skills* as we explore [Next Theme Title].</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>